{
  "id": "Compound::DB00091 - Disease::DOID:8893",
  "verbalization": "Cyclosporine is implicated in the pathogenesis of Psoriasis through a complex network of interactions involving immunosuppressive mechanisms. The evidence suggests that Cyclosporine causes Renal Graft Loss or Renal Abscess, both of which are subsequently treated by Mycophenolate Mofetil, which in turn treats Psoriasis. This indicates a potential pathway where Cyclosporine and Mycophenolate Mofetil, both immunosuppressants, may influence the immune system in a manner that contributes to Psoriasis. The shared ancestry of Cyclosporine and Mycophenolate Mofetil as organic amino compounds highlights a common biological substrate, reinforcing their role in immunosuppression. The relationship between Cyclosporine and Psoriasis is thus mediated through the immunosuppressive effects of these compounds, though the exact mechanism of action remains to be fully elucidated.",
